          equipment: '',
          dealing: 'Dealer shuffles, player to left cuts. Deal in packets of 6 cards: one to each player, one to talon (middle), repeat. Each player receives 12 cards, talon has 6. Play is anticlockwise.',
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: "In positive contracts, declarer's side wins by taking at least 36 of 70 card points. Various contracts have different objectives (avoid tricks, win exact number of tricks, etc.).",
          gameplay: "Point-trick game with bidding. Players bid contracts; highest bidder becomes declarer. In many contracts declarer calls a king to choose a secret partner. Declarer may exchange cards with the six-card talon. After announcements (bonuses, kontras), forehand leads to the first trick. Players must follow suit if possible, otherwise must play a trump. In positive contracts declarer's side needs at least 36 of 70 card points to win; negative contracts have different objectives (e.g. lose every trick in Bettel).",
          keyRules: [
            "Follow suit if possible; if not, must play a trump. In positive contracts declarer's side wins with 36+ of 70 points. Talon: 6 cards; declarer may take 3 and discard 3 in Rufer-type contracts. Call a king to choose partner (holder is secret). Trull cards: Sküs, XXI (Mond), I (lowest trump) worth 5 points each. Play is anticlockwise; forehand leads first in positive contracts.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: 'In positive contracts, aim for 36+ points. Protect high-value cards (kings, honours). Remember the SkÃ¼s, XXI, and I are each worth 5 points plus their trick value.',
          intermediate: "Talon selection is crucial. Look for packets containing kings or high trumps. When calling a king, call one you don't have - if it appears in talon, you play alone (often disadvantageous).",
          advanced: "Bird Strategy: Announcing Pagat Ultimo, Uhu, Kakadu, or Marabu doubles their value but commits you to a specific play. Only announce if you have strong position. In negative contracts (Bettel, Piccolo), card play becomes inverted - you're trying to lose tricks, which requires understanding opponents' hands.",
          tips: [
            'The Mond (XXI) is vulnerable - if you hold it, be aware an opponent may try to capture it with SkÃ¼s.',
            'In Rufer, your partner is unknown until the called king appears - play accordingly.',
            'Valat (winning all tricks) is rare but lucrative - consider going for it with very strong hands.',
            'Card counting is essential - track which trumps have been played.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
